From d075178926c300731795adb4034121fb5173063a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Graham Taylor-Jones <graham.taylor-jones@cargt.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 10:50:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] stm32-stgen: read RTC before disabling counter on suspend

Root cause of the poweroff/IWDG-reset bug (customer report:
"poweroff behaves like reboot"), found via checkpoint tracing
through the whole TF-A -> OP-TEE system-off path
(0003/0004 diagnostic patches): every registered PM_OP_SUSPEND
driver callback traced cleanly except stm32-stgen, whose suspend
handler never returns.

stm32_stgen_pm_suspend() disabled the STGEN counter - which
directly feeds the ARM generic timer (CNTPCT) - before calling
stm32_rtc_get_calendar(). That function polls an RTC
"registers synchronized" hardware flag with a safety-net
timeout measured using that same generic timer
(timeout_init_us()/timeout_elapsed()). With STGEN already
disabled, that timeout's clock is frozen: if the RTC sync flag
isn't already set at the instant of the call, the poll spins
forever instead of timing out and panicking cleanly - silently,
with no further trace or log output, until the independent
hardware watchdog (a separate clock domain) eventually fires and
resets the board. This also explains the bug's intermittent
nature: it only manifests when the RTC sync flag isn't already
set at that exact moment.

Read the RTC calendar first, while the timer is still running,
so its own timeout can still function (and panic with a clear
message, as designed, if the RTC genuinely doesn't respond)
before disabling the counter that timeout depends on.

Signed-off-by: Graham Taylor-Jones <graham.taylor-jones@cargt.com>
---
 core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c | 21 +++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c b/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c
index 938944d6b..63a8b07e9 100644
--- a/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c
+++ b/core/drivers/counter/stm32_stgen.c
@@ -89,15 +89,24 @@
 	uint64_t counter_val = 0;
 
 	/*
+	 * Save current time from the RTC before disabling the STGEN
+	 * counter below: stm32_rtc_get_calendar() polls a hardware
+	 * "registers synchronized" flag with a timeout measured using the
+	 * ARM generic timer, which STGEN feeds. Disabling STGEN first
+	 * freezes that timeout's clock, so if the RTC sync flag isn't
+	 * already set, the poll's safety-net timeout can never elapse and
+	 * it spins forever instead of timing out - eventually caught only
+	 * by the independent hardware watchdog resetting the board.
+	 */
+	if (stm32_rtc_get_calendar(stgen_d.calendar))
+		panic("Could not get RTC calendar at suspend");
+
+	/*
 	 * Disable STGEN counter. At this point, it should be stopped by
 	 * software.
 	 */
 	io_clrbits32(stgen_d.base + STGENC_CNTCR, BIT(0));
 
-	/* Save current time from the RTC */
-	if (stm32_rtc_get_calendar(stgen_d.calendar))
-		panic("Could not get RTC calendar at suspend");
-
 	/* Save current counter value */
 	counter_val = stm32_stgen_get_counter_value();
 	stgen_d.cnt_h = (uint32_t)(counter_val >> 32);
--
